Bernhard Schick received the degree in mechatronic engineering from the University of Applied Sciences Heilbronn, Heilbronn, Germany. From 1994, whilst at TÜV SÜD, he built up his expertise in the ﬁeld of vehicle dynamics and advanced driver assistance systems, in various positions up to a general Manager. He joined IPG Automotive in 2007 as managing Director, where he worked in the ﬁeld of vehicle dynamics simulation. From 2014 he was responsible for calibration and virtual testing technologies as global business unit Manager at AVL List, Graz. Since 2016, he has been a Research Professor with the University of Applied Sciences Kempten, Kempten, Germany, and the Head of the Institute for Driver Assistance Systems and Connected Mobility. His research focus is automated driving and vehicle dynamics.

Maximilian Böhle received the B.Sc. and M.Sc. degrees in automotive engineering from the Munich University of Applied Sciences, Munich, Germany, in 2016 and 2019, respectively. He is currently working toward the Doctoral degree in automotive engineering with the Faculty of Mechanical Engineering and Transport Systems of the Technical University of Berlin, Berlin, Germany. Since 2020, he has been a Research Assistant with the Institute for Driver Assistance and Connected Mobility, Kempten University of Applied Sciences, Kempten, Germany. His research interests include steering feel and vehicle dynamics for driving simulators.

Steffen Müller received the Dipl.-Ing. degree in astronautics and aerospace engineering and the Dr.Ing. degree from the Technical University of Berlin, Berlin, Germany, in 1993 and 1998, respectively. From 1998 to 2000, he was a Project Manager at the ABB Corporate Research Center, Heidelberg, Germany. He ﬁnished his post-doctoral research with the University of California, Berkeley, Berkeley, CA, USA, in 2001. From 2001 to 2008, he held leading positions at the BMW Research and Innovation Centre. From 2008 to 2013, he was the Founder and leader of the Chair for mechatronics in engineering and vehicle technology with Technical University of Kaiserslautern, Kaiserslautern, Germany. He is currently a University Professor and an Einstein Professor with the Technical University of Berlin, Berlin, Germany. He is the Head of the Chair of Automotive Engineering, Faculty of Mechanical Engineering and Transport Systems.

